\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What the Experiment Shows}

The experiment separates three claims.

First, the fast and deliberate pathways have genuinely different capabilities. The linear fast baseline fails on Hard inputs, while the MLP succeeds. This is not a memorization artifact; it follows from the function class.

Second, the monitor learns a non-trivial routing policy. Deliberation rises from a few percent on Easy inputs to nearly all Medium and Hard inputs. The ablation below shows that this policy depends on the memory readout: without it, the monitor still deliberates at the same overall rate but misallocates deliberation on the inputs where it matters.

Third, ACP matches always-deliberate accuracy while deliberating on two-thirds of inputs. The saving in expensive-path invocations comes from skipping deliberation on easy inputs, which the fast pathway can already solve.

\subsection{Does the Novelty Feature Matter?}

The monitor in the main experiment sees both the input and the episodic novelty score. To isolate the contribution of the memory readout, we retrain ACP with the novelty term removed, so the monitor sees only $x$. Table~\ref{tab:ablation} reports the result over three seeds.

\begin{table}[htbp]
