\midrule
\input{charts/ablation_table.tex}
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The x-only monitor still learns a similar overall deliberation rate, 66.2 percent versus 67.2 percent, but it allocates that deliberation poorly. It deliberates on 11.2 percent of Easy inputs and only 87.9 percent of Hard inputs. The Hard misroutes are expensive: the linear fast path cannot fit the product target, so a small number of them pulls task accuracy down from 94.3 to 73.7 percent. The memory feature is therefore not decorative. It stabilizes the routing decision at the margin where an error costs the most.

\subsection{Cost of the Monitor}

Deliberation rate is not the same as total compute. The monitor scans $K=30$ memory keys of dimension 16 on every input, which costs about 496 multiply-accumulates, compared with about 864 for the deliberate MLP. At the observed deliberation rate of 67 percent, the total cost is therefore not lower than always-deliberate. The routing policy is near-optimal, but the current memory readout is too expensive to turn that policy into a net saving. A memory of a few keys, or a low-dimensional sketch, would move the crossover. We report deliberation rate as the routing metric and leave wall-clock optimization to future work.

\subsection{Limitations}

The task is synthetic and low-dimensional. The memory is fixed and hand-filled; it acts as a novelty detector rather than a content-addressable episodic store. The ablation shows that this scalar signal carries real routing information, but a deployed system would need learned writes and content-based retrieval. The deliberate pathway is a single MLP, not an iterative or search-based reasoner. Routing supervision uses ground-truth labels, which a deployed system would not have. Future work should address these issues.

\subsection{Toward Human-Like Systems}

Humans do not think for minutes on every question. They answer quickly when the situation is familiar, slow down when something is off, and can often explain why they slowed down. ACP captures a minimal version of this pattern. The fast pathway answers by default. The monitor uses memory to decide when to escalate. The routing flag makes the decision inspectable.

This is a small step toward AI that allocates computation the way people do: fluent by default, deliberate when necessary.

\section{Conclusion}

We presented Adaptive Cognitive Processing, a minimal architecture for input-dependent computation. A linear fast pathway answers by default. A memory-aware monitor triggers a deliberate MLP when the input is novel. We implemented the system in NumPy, verified the gradients numerically, and demonstrated on a synthetic task that the monitor learns a clear routing gradient while matching always-deliberate accuracy and deliberating on only two-thirds of inputs. An ablation shows that removing the memory readout drops task accuracy from 94.3 to 73.7 percent, so the novelty signal is doing real work.

The code is open source at \url{https://github.com/MenteE-s/adaptive-cognitive-processing}. Future work will extend the architecture to language tasks, learned memory, iterative deliberation, and embodied settings.

